{"parentUuid":"9b9c3078-f2ee-4101-8ebf-522b65d5587d","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a084f4b977ecf6d4a","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5","id":"msg_011Cg22n4QbnRxAZRJKM7NjA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SEhzp46pP1j61MRrMRuvvt","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"MIL diagnostics built, tested, committed and pushed. Commit hash: 2210d76cb5f5c4faf41bc2d447a0032882ad6ecf (pushed to `github` main only).\n\nRoot cause found (task 1): `mes.workup.build()`'s clear-assessment was anchored to the latest FES engineering session (`FESLog_2610041116...txt`, 2026-10-04). The 2026-10-08 09:20 clear was SCAN-only (`SCAN_2610080920.txt`) with no FES session after it at all, so it fell entirely outside that anchor's lookback window and the dossier kept reporting the stale 2026-09-29 clear. A secondary parsing wrinkle: the Engine/ECM clear-result block appears twice in that file (an incomplete first attempt, then a real SUCCESS later) and the second SUCCESS line sits one blank line further down than the parser's per-module read stops, so it came back blank — naively that would have misfiled ECM as \"refused.\" Fixed both in `mes-log-mcp/mes/workup.py`: added `_newest_global_clear()` (scans the whole corpus, FES or SCAN, for the newest actual clear event, not just the FES-anchored window) and resolved blank clear results from evidence (whether the module's own pre-clear codes are confirmed gone on the next read) rather than from the literal string. Verified against the real corpus: `cleared_at` now correctly resolves to `2026-10-08 09:20:00`.\n\nBuilt per spec:\n- (a) Recorded the owner's attested statement via `owner_events.add()` (incidents/warning_light_seen, by owner, fields_meta typed) — written to the real production store at `C:\\ProgramData\\cuore\\owner_events.jsonl`.\n- (b) Additive MIL section in `cuore/services/dossier_bridge.py` (`_build_mil_block` + helpers) comparing the ECM's from-car MIL-commanded state (readiness observation, 2026-10-13 20:03) against the owner's attested dash-lamp report, rendered in one additive block each on `cuore/web/templates/bench.html` and `report.html`, wired through `bench_bridge.py`'s verdict dict.\n- (c) Three new entries in `mes-log-mcp/mes/mechanic_tests.py`: `mil_bulb_check`, `mil_request_vs_lamp` (SAE J1979 for the PID $01 MIL bit), `ipc_mil_status_read` (honestly UNKNOWN — no IPC DID is catalogued in `cuore/live/did_catalog.py` yet, so it's marked \"not polled\").\n- (d) The mismatch is surfaced as a hedged finding (\"consistent with\") on both the MIL block and Bench's next-action; never asserts which explanation is right.\n\nNew test `cuore/tests/check_mil.py` (self-contained, isolated `CUORE_STATE_DIR`/`MES_LOG_DIR`, copies the real `SCAN_2610080920/26.txt` when present else a baked-in fallback, plus a synthetic older FES to reproduce the exact stale-anchor bug): 60/60 checks pass. Also ran and confirmed green: `check_bench.py` (74/74), `check_dossier_page.py` (42/42), `check_tests_page.py` (85/85), `check_screens.py` (28/28).\n\nFiles touched (mine only — left all other agents' concurrent changes to catalog.py/electrical.py/faulttree.py/system_detail_bridge.py/experience files/server.py untouched and unstaged):\n- C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\mes-log-mcp\\mes\\workup.py\n- C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\mes-log-mcp\\mes\\mechanic_tests.py\n- C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\cuore\\services\\dossier_bridge.py\n- C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\cuore\\services\\bench_bridge.py\n- C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\cuore\\web\\templates\\bench.html\n- C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\cuore\\web\\templates\\report.html\n- C:\\Users\\User\\mcp-servers\\cuore\\tests\\check_mil.py (new)\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: \"distance with MIL 34 km\" (Mode 01 PID $21) has no dedicated field in the live-observation schema yet (`since_clear` only carries `mil_on`/`stored_dtc_count`/monitors) — I recorded it as a small sourced literal (`_MIL_DISTANCE_KM` in dossier_bridge.py) tied to this VIN/session rather than inventing a schema field, with a comment flagging it for a future PID $21 reader."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":493,"cache_read_input_tokens":285170,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":493,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01SEhzp46pP1j61MRrMRuvvt":{"message":"MIL diagnostics built, tested, committed and pushed. Commit hash: 2210d76cb5f5c4faf41bc2d447a0032882ad6ecf (pushed to `github` main only).\n\nRoot cause found (task 1): `mes.workup.build()`'s clear-assessment was anchored to the latest FES engineering session (`FESLog_2610041116...txt`, 2026-10-04). The 2026-10-08 09:20 clear was SCAN-only (`SCAN_2610080920.txt`) with no FES session after it at all, so it fell entirely outside that anchor's lookback window and the dossier kept reporting the stale 2026-09-29 clear. A secondary parsing wrinkle: the Engine/ECM clear-result block appears twice in that file (an incomplete first attempt, then a real SUCCESS later) and the second SUCCESS line sits one blank line further down than the parser's per-module read stops, so it came back blank — naively that would have misfiled ECM as \"refused.\" Fixed both in `mes-log-mcp/mes/workup.py`: added `_newest_global_clear()` (scans the whole corpus, FES or SCAN, for the newest actual clear event, not just the FES-anchored window) and resolved blank clear results from evidence (whether the module's own pre-clear codes are confirmed gone on the next read) rather than from the literal string. Verified against the real corpus: `cleared_at` now correctly resolves to `2026-10-08 09:20:00`.\n\nBuilt per spec:\n- (a) Recorded the owner's attested statement via `owner_events